[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=18368)[0m John Douglas SJ (11 May 1769 – 26 August 1824) was an Irish cardinal of the Roman Catholic Church. He was the first Irish cardinal and only the tenth in Scotland to see his conclave called at the department of Cardinal Camerlengo. Douglas was a co-founder of the Society of Jesus himself at the age of 30 in Glasgow in 1699. Douglas was involved in the injury of Alcuin Ward who was under a house arrest by British government in the penal era. Douglas wrote several scholarly works, in poetry, essays, and sermons. The first, An Essay on the Reale Character and Duties of a Model Knight, was published in 1707. The second, A Closes Reserve, The Mexican Valley, A Secret Army at Flushing, The Death of a Minor-Was set to Stage, By T. Colston, in 1800. It was a theatrical work during Douglas' refectory.
