[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=18379)[0m The United States Air Force Test Pilot School (USAF TPS) is a graduate school within the Air Force centered on the Air Force Flight Test Center at Edwards Air Force Base, California. Conducted at the Flight Test Center, the school is supported by the Air Force Flight Test Engineering Center. The USAF TPS is based at Edwards AFB, California with secondary facilities located at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base, Ohio. Established in 1947 as the Elizabeth Doud School of Experimental Flight Test, TPS replaced the USAF Flight Test Pilot School, which had been founded nearly two decades earlier. Throughout its history, the USAF TPS had a principled mission to train the USAF personnel and the military members of allied nations who would operate, test, and evaluate the most advanced military aircraft, engines, avionics, and other sensor systems. The TPS students are named medalists of the US PT, and PT-19 tail number is painted on the student's aircraft for identification. (The last flight test class graduated in June 2018, and the USAF TPS is currently transitioning through the remaining curricula into a new curricular arrangement and date within 2023.)
